{"name":"Lime Sherbet","hex":"#cdd78a","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"lime","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any inorganic material containing calcium, usually calcium oxide (quicklime) or calcium hydroxide (slaked lime).","Any gluey or adhesive substance that traps or captures; sometimes a synonym for birdlime."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To treat with calcium hydroxide or calcium oxide (lime).","To smear with birdlime.\n To ensnare, catch, entrap."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/lime"},{"term":"sherbet","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A dessert of frozen fruit juice with a dairy product such as milk added; a sorbet with dairy ingredients.","An effervescent powder made of bicarbonate of soda, sugar and flavourings, intended to be eaten alone or mixed with water to make a drink."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sherbet"}],"entries":[{"title":"Sherbet (frozen dessert)","blocks":[{"text":"Sherbet, sometimes referred to as sherbert, is a frozen dessert made from water, sugar, a dairy product such as cream or milk, and a flavoring – typically fruit juice or purée, wine, liqueur, or occasionally non-fruit flavors such as vanilla, chocolate, or peppermint. It is similar to, but distinct from, sorbet, which lacks dairy. Sherbet has 1%–2% milkfat, much less than ice cream."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/eb/Rainbow_Sherbet_%2838251341974%29.jpg/330px-Rainbow_Sherbet_%2838251341974%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sherbet_(frozen_dessert)","wikidata":"Q107339876"},{"title":"Sherbet (powder)","blocks":[{"text":"Sherbet, also called Sherbert or Soda powder, is a fizzy, sweet powdered sugar, usually eaten by dipping a lollipop or liquorice stick, using a small spoon, or licking it from a finger."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7b/Jars_of_Kali_or_Sherbet_powder.jpg/330px-Jars_of_Kali_or_Sherbet_powder.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sherbet_(powder)","wikidata":"Q1262538"},{"title":"Lime (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Lime is a color that is a shade of yellow-green, so named because it is a representation of the color of the citrus fruit lime. It is the color that is in between the web color chartreuse and yellow on the color wheel. Alternate names for this color included yellow-green, lemon-lime, lime green, or bitter lime."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c2/AosaHGkyuai_%28cropped%29_3.jpg/330px-AosaHGkyuai_%28cropped%29_3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lime_(color)","wikidata":"Q906936"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/Brausepulver.jpg/500px-Brausepulver.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/Brausepulver.jpg/500px-Brausepulver.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"German Brausepulver is similar, and while originally sold  as such is often not mixed with water nowadays but eaten by children by dipping a wet finger into it, or by adults in combination with vodka.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABrausepulver.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a7/Sherbet_lemons_1.jpg/500px-Sherbet_lemons_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a7/Sherbet_lemons_1.jpg/1280px-Sherbet_lemons_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a7/Sherbet_lemons_1.jpg/500px-Sherbet_lemons_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.o